  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Six-foot waves smashed Japan out of a completely calm night. In January 1700, ocean surges battered villages\u2014without any earthquake felt beforehand.",
        "visual_keywords": "stormy ocean waves night"
      },
      {
        "narration": "For centuries, samurai logs called it the orphan tsunami. No volcano had erupted, and zero earthquakes were felt in Asia.",
        "visual_keywords": "ancient paper manuscript scroll"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Then geologists found ghost forests across the Pacific in North America. What killed an entire cedar forest at that exact same hour?",
        "visual_keywords": "foggy dead forest trees"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Saltwater flooded them when the Cascadia fault violently snapped. A magnitude 9.0 megathrust earthquake dropped the American coastline six feet.",
        "visual_keywords": "cracked ground earth fissure"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Dendrochronology dated the tree deaths to the 1699 growing season, whereas Japanese tsunami records and wave reconstructions pinpointed the exact date and time to around 9:00 PM on January 26, 1700.",
        "visual_keywords": "tree trunk cross section"
      }
